// SPDX-License-Identifier: (MIT OR Apache-2.0)
// Compile Error: a type that contains itself with nothing in between
// Rejects: `Node` contains itself, so it has no size
//
// Every field is stored inline, so a `Node` holding a `Node` needs room for
// the one inside it, which needs room for the one inside that. No size works.
//
// The checker used to accept all of this and leave it to layout, which
// shrugged — `warning: unknown type 'Node' in layout, defaulting to pointer
// size (8, 8)` — and then what happened depended on luck (#1280):
//
//   - `enum Tree { Node(Tree) }` ran and printed the right answer, because the
//     program never stored a `Node` payload. Any program that did would have
//     written through a layout eight bytes wide.
//   - `struct Node { next: Node? }` reached MIR and died there: "field `next`
//     holds 32 bytes but its slot is 16 … That is a compiler bug". The backend
//     was right about the damage and three passes too late to name the cause.
//
// The message is also the rung of the decision ladder that was missing. This
// is exactly the wall where an author needs to be told which door opens it:
// `Heap<T>` for a single-owner recursive value, `Rack` + `Link<T>?` for nodes
// that point at each other or need identity.

// ERROR: a struct field is inline, and so is the `?` around it.
struct Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

// ERROR: a variant payload is inline too.
enum Tree {
    Leaf(i32)
    Node(Tree)
}

// ERROR: a tuple doesn't break it either.
struct Pair {
    both: (i64, Pair)
}

// Through another type, which is the case a per-field name check can't see.
// ERROR: `Edge` holds a `Vertex`, and `Vertex` holds an `Edge`.
struct Edge {
    to: Vertex
}

struct Vertex {
    id: i64
    out: Edge?
}

func main() {
    println("unreachable")
}
